\title{baux documentation}
\meta{toc}{false}

\p{baux is a black-box optimization library with a Rust core and a Python API. Define a parameter space, evaluate the points it proposes, and return the results. Your function does not need to provide gradients.}

\subtree{
  \title{Installation}
  \p{Install the Python package:}
  \pre{
python -m pip install baux
  }
  \p{The [published wheels](https://pypi.org/project/baux/#files) support Python 3.12–3.15 on Linux x86-64 and macOS Apple Silicon.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Usage}
  \<div>[id]{quickstart}{
  \pre{
import baux
import numpy as np

bounds = np.array([[-2.0, 2.0], [-2.0, 2.0]])
state = baux.init(bounds, pipe=baux.enn(), seed=0)

for _ in range(10):
    state, batch = baux.ask(state, n=4)
    values = -np.sum(batch.points**2, axis=1)
    state, _ = baux.tell(state, batch, values)

print(state.best_x, state.best_y)
  }
  }
  \p{baux maximizes values. This example returns a negative squared distance to minimize the distance from the origin. Keep the state returned by \code{ask} and \code{tell} for the next call.}
  \p{The [quickstart](quickstart.html) explains the loop. The [optimizer reference](docs.html) documents its inputs and outputs.}
}

